.TH CTPOOL_CONSTRUCT_SCOPED 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ctpool_construct_scoped \- declare, initialise, and auto-destroy a thread pool
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cthreadpool.h>

ctpool_construct_scoped(name, num_threads, queue_capacity);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Combines ctpool_declare_scoped() and the initialisation performed by
ctpool_construct() into a single statement. The pool is automatically
destroyed (with an implicit drain shutdown if one was not already
called) when the variable leaves scope.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name            Variable name for the pool handle.
num_threads     Number of worker threads to spawn (must be >= 1).
queue_capacity  Maximum pending tasks (0 or ccol_invalid_size for
                unbounded).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Calls fatal_err() on failure; use create_cthread_pool() directly when
graceful error handling is required.
Requires GCC or Clang (uses __attribute__((cleanup))).
The implicit drain inside the destructor may block for a long time if
tasks are slow; call ctpool_shutdown_drain() or
ctpool_shutdown_immediate() explicitly before the end of scope when
bounded latency on exit matters.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
void process_batch(void) {
    ctpool_construct_scoped(pool, 4, 0);
    for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++)
        ctpool_submit(pool, work_fn, items[i], NULL);
    ctpool_shutdown_drain(pool);
}  /* pool destroyed automatically */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ctpool_construct (3),
.BR ctpool_declare_scoped (3),
.BR ___ctpool_destroy (3),
.BR ctpool_shutdown_drain (3),
.BR ctpool_shutdown_immediate (3)
